00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:43.160
was essentially using those coding agents as an accelerator to writing code.

245
00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:46.960
Come the Friday, we're essentially trying to get as much done as we possibly can

246
00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:49.280
looking backwards on that week.

247
00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:51.800
Whereas today with the Dark Factory,

248
00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:53.440
what we're actually doing is it's a bad Friday.

249
00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:54.680
If we don't prep the weekend,

250
00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:58.440
we're actually looking forward to think, can I plan and stack

251
00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:02.840
and queue enough for the Dark Factory to have enough work going forward?

252
00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:06.600
It's a very different mindset in terms of you're essentially managing

253
00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:10.640
the agent work versus, you know, essentially feeding

254
00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:15.120
on a minute to minute basis the agent with tasks to do. Yep.

255
00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:16.000
Yeah, definitely.

256
00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.640
And it's a little bit of that shift in my mind.

257
00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:20.760
Like you're shifting up the abstraction stack a bit.

258
00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:22.080
You're no longer thinking about like,

259
00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.560
what is this ticket that I'm going to try to get done?

260
00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.560
You're thinking about what is this feature that I'm going to I want to do?

261
00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:30.200
What is the sequence of steps that someone needs to do it,

262
00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:32.120
just like I divvy up to a junior engineer

263
00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:34.080
or an intern or something along those lines.

264
00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:37.440
And then how do I make sure that the stuff that I care about, either

265
00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:40.040
like the correctness or the UI verification,

266
00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:43.080
or the clicking the buttons or hitting the endpoints or whatever

267
00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.440
it is, that kind of eye is important to me.

268
00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:46.960
How do I make sure that's encoded in a way

269
00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:50.800
where I trust that it's going to get done and the agent is going to do it?

270
00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:53.040
And so I think it's both for me

271
00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.720
kind of like that stacking up and that forward looking aspect of it.

272
00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:59.720
But the other big shift has been kind of what

273
00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:02.800
we now have these agents, these models

274
00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:07.480
where we can literally deterministically load context into their context window

275
00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:09.760
to enforce the fact that they've read something.

276
00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:11.600
It's very hard for me to get a human to go read

277
00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:14.600
a doc on best practices or principles or something along those lines.

278
00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:17.840
So I have this additional leverage from being able to say,

279
00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:21.120
you know, that I want to build the code, a codebase in this way.

280
00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:23.320
So you're going to go build it in that way.

281
00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:24.320
And I

282
00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:27.400
therefore have a higher level of trust in the code that's going to be outputted.

283
00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:31.240
I can then also start layering in other bits of verification around

284
00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:34.840
like coding styles, or around testing, or around enforcement or stuff

285
00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:35.600
along those lines.

286
00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:38.280
And this is all about to that feeding the qubit.

287
00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:42.200
The only way that I would trust to be able to build a queue that will

288
00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:45.800
then sequence and run autonomously is if I have enough trust that the output of

289
00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:49.880
that queue isn't at a bare minimum going to break existing functionality,

290
00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:53.840
but ideally is going to be something that maybe not proud of writing myself.

291
00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:56.600
But I would be okay if I had written it myself.

292
00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:57.360
Yeah.

293
00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:03.320
Let's, let's jump through what a Dark Factory is, component by component.

294
00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:06.400
And and I'd love for you to walk us through

295
00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:11.240
how we started on this Dark Factory journey, what we implemented first.

296
00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:14.600
And yeah, you know, really understanding that path

297
00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:18.800
so that others can kind of follow and understand what they should do first.

298
00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:20.480
So when we first

299
00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:24.200
tried to build this Dark Factory, what were the initial steps that we took.

300
00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:29.200
So I think there to kind of set the lay of the land a bit.

301
00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:31.760
There's a couple important concepts here.

302
00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:36.240
One is the orchestrator, which is what's actually picking up tickets

303
00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.400
and then passing it off to coding agents

304
00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:39.040
doing work

305
00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:41.080
and doing the whole babysitting thing in terms of making

306
00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.000
sure the PR goes through and all that kind of stuff.

307
00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:47.280
But then importantly and we'll touch on the second is the actual context

308
00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.680
and environment within our repo that we give to the coding agents,

309
00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:53.720
which I personally think is where the higher amount of leverage comes from.

310
00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:57.640
But we built the first thing first, the orchestrator first,

311
00:14:57.640 --> 00:14:59.120
just because that is the tractable thing.

312
00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:01.040
That's kind of what we think of when we think of it.

313
00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:05.040
Ark Software Factory is the mechanism to go from linear tickets

314
00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:07.200
to actually produce code.

315
00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:10.200
I think just one of our big learnings previewing this has been

316
00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:13.280
that is dead simple and not that hard.

317
00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:17.480
It's everything else that surrounds that where you then because if you don't

318
00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:21.160
kind of invest in those other layers, you then back in that previous workflow case

319
00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:22.000
we were talking about

320
00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.120
where you stand up overwhelmed with review or you end up overwhelmed with code,

321
00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:28.560
or you end up doing things that aren't actually driving the whole system forward.

322
00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:29.800
So the orchestrator isn't here,

323
00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:33.040
an agent orchestrator, it's essentially almost like a workflow orchestra.

324
00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:33.520
Exactly.

325
00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:35.000
It's it's connecting.

326
00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:36.800
It's an integrator between agents

327
00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:40.800
and other tools or other things like linear as a good example.

328
00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:41.840
Exactly.

329
00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:47.560
And so our journey started with basically a Python queue that connected to linear

330
00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:52.120
and connected to GitHub and ran an agent locally in a Docker sandbox.

331
00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:55.000
And we literally had one of our engine not allowed to close our laptop

332
00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:55.720
for about a week

333
00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:59.600
because it was already locally on our machine, and we had much more adoption

334
00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:02.600
on that that than we had expected internally there.

335
00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:05.480
And so kind of the design decisions that we made around

336
00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:08.960
that were trying to keep things as simple as possible

337
00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:12.520
and relying on other systems as their source of truth, where we could,

338
00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:14.600
because we didn't want to have to reinvent the wheel there.

339
00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:16.560
So we've made an opinion choice.

340
00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.480
Linear is our source of truth ought to be done.

341
00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:23.240
Everything must flow through linear if it's going to result in a durable artifact

342
00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:24.600
that is oriented,

343
00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:27.720
that is a pure in our codebase, or a notion doc,

344
00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:30.520
or any of the other artifacts that are produced by the Dark Factory,

345
00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:35.640
that just allows us the audit trail, the debugging, the visibility on that.

346
00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:36.520
And it means that we can

347
00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:40.160
then rely on all the work that linear is done in terms of blocking relationships,

348
00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:43.960
subtasks, tagging for the reporting and the mechanisms that we need there.

349
00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:45.720
And we don't need to build it ourselves.

350
00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:48.560
So we have basically a pole.

351
00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:50.360
We're working to shift this over to webhooks.

352
00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:52.120
It just hasn't been a top of the priority list,

353
00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:55.120
because we haven't broken linear yet with our polling other than our tests,

354
00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:56.240
which I'll mention later.

355
00:16:56.240 --> 00:17:00.240
So we have something that polls linear for new work delegated to factory

356
00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:03.280
and state to do, picks it up, puts it onto the queue.

357
00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:07.120
There's a bunch of prioritization logic depending on kind of which project it's

358
00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:10.200
coming from, what the priority is, whether it's being blocked, stuff

359
00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:15.040
along those lines that then gets farmed out to a coding agent.

360
00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:18.920
As I mentioned originally, that was just running in Docker on someone's machine.

361
00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:20.760
We just really want an isolated environment,

362
00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:23.880
because we don't want these agents to kind of be interacting with each other,

363
00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:28.040
because each one is meant to be kind of a one shot to implementation.

364
00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:31.680
Mind you, they can technically understand what other agents are going on.

365
00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:33.640
And we do see this in the logs, because we'll

366
00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:36.600
often reference other tickets in the description of a ticket.

367
00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:38.640
And so an agent will go check in on

368
00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:41.400
how that ticket is going or what the PR was associated with that.

369
00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:43.800
But that's just kind of an emergent behavior in terms of building up

370
00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:46.800
the context needed to be able to do effective implementation.

371
00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:51.320
These agents currently, for us run in a sandbox, we're using Daytona

372
00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:53.880
just because of the isolation and security guarantees there.

373
00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:55.760
This runs in our own internal Daytona

374
00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:59.960
environments, and we provision a relatively beefy box for that.

375
00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.000
Technically VM in order to technically container actually

376
00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:07.760
in order to allow it to spin up the entire mono repo testing stack.

377
00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:10.760
So basically everything that a local engineer can do on their

378
00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:13.800
machine modulo creds that the local engineer has access

379
00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.800
to, that we don't give to the agent we want the agent to be able to do.

380
00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:20.560
This allows the agent to run our full test suite, including integration tests.

381
00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:25.760
Spin up the repo, click around on a UI, click around on basically anything that we

382
00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:29.960
would be doing for validation and testing, all in their own virtual environment.

383
00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:34.480
That helps us build kind of confidence there when really interesting

384
00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:37.600
benefit of that is that we've now been able to have the agent post videos

385
00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:41.000
and screenshots back onto the PR so that we, as humans can go

386
00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:44.800
manually verify the work without needing to actually SSH on the box and go

387
00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:45.960
click the buttons ourselves.

388
00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:49.360
We can see what the flow looks like from that video, from that evidence,

389
00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:49.840
which helps

390
00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:53.480
then build trust and build confidence in that it implemented the right thing.

391
00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:57.560
That is kind of the crux of the orchestration flow.

392
00:18:57.560 --> 00:19:01.840
The other bit of the orchestrator is just basically babysitting and PR.

393
00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:07.560
So once the coding agent is done, pass a couple of hooks, pushes up a PR,

394
00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:11.680
we're relatively confident that PR at least like passes lint builds, passes

395
00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:17.160
tests, like it's a good agent for short implementation on the PR surface.

396
00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:18.880
So this is now we're in GitHub.

397
00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:20.920
We were in two different coding agents.

398
00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:22.040
One is Code Rabbit.

399
00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:24.160
One is our own internal code review agents.

400
00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:27.000
Our internal code review agents kind of is anchored on skills.

401
00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.320
So we have written three at the moment.

402
00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:32.680
Other ones coming soon, skills that are targeting different

403
00:19:32.680 --> 00:19:36.360
bits of kind of the way that we what our code review philosophy is.

404
00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:38.040
So we're on our own security

405
00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:41.880
one around legibility, which is really is this code easy to maintain in the long

406
00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:45.640
term and one around kind of catch all functional correctness.

407
00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:49.920
We then so we run our own code review tool.

408
00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:52.760
This is what's exposed via tehsil change review.

409
00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:56.920
And we are using that as signals on the PR for the agent to go fix.

410
00:19:56.920 --> 00:20:02.200
So Code Rabbit and our internal tool post comments back on the PR

411
00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:06.400
and we have failures in CI when some tests don't pass or

412
00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:10.480
some we do some what's called mutation testing, property based testing,

413
00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:11.400
a whole bunch of that kind of stuff.

414
00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:14.560
If those don't pass, those all get posted back as signals on the PR,

415
00:20:14.720 --> 00:20:19.120
the orchestrator is watching the PR, and so we'll rework the agent

416
00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:23.000
when those comments are passed back and the agent then goes fixes them.

417
00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:25.720
It will take in the comments. It will reason over them.

418
00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:29.240
It then responds inline in those comments were appropriate

419
00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:33.000
or is a top level comments resolving where appropriate as needed

