00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:46.160
back on the linear ticket, which then notifies the human.

424
00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:50.440
And so that is kind of the flow in a nutshell, going from a linear ticket

425
00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:53.800
to orchestrator to coding agent on box to pair

426
00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:57.000
to a bunch of cycles on the PR review between automated bots

427
00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:02.240
and CI checks back to the agent and till it gets to a state that is good.

428
00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:06.800
That's normally when we flip to in review, and we'll get the human

429
00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:09.520
in order to engage with that, because it's kind of like in

430
00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:11.800
hopefully the best state that it could possibly be.

431
00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:14.920
And to human, the interaction mode there is twofold.

432
00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:16.720
One, we do have the escape hatch.

433
00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:19.720
If they can pull down the branch locally, make changes

434
00:21:19.720 --> 00:21:22.160
and then push it back up, that's always an option

435
00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:25.320
if it's going to be quicker or they need to do it that way.

436
00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:29.040
But we're trying to push for, though, is doing the interaction mode on that PR

437
00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:32.880
surface via comments, because that then allows us to kind of track

438
00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:37.400
that and also integrate that back into kind of what our review philosophy is

439
00:21:37.400 --> 00:21:39.240
that makes it serviceable, actionable,

440
00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:43.280
so that we can kind of continue to leverage agents or continue to leverage

441
00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:47.120
those insights into better review flows so that we don't need to get a human

442
00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:48.080
to identify that stuff again.

443
00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:51.640
And that's just pull requests that do require a human interaction.

444
00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:53.760
Exactly. For for a review. Exactly. Awesome.

445
00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:56.720
So the orchestrator in this sense actually does sound like kind of

446
00:21:56.720 --> 00:21:59.320
what a human would do today in that kind of flow.

447
00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:02.760
So it's pretty much and let's talk a little bit about each step there.

448
00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:07.200
Because, you know, if we were to ask an agent

449
00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:09.520
to do various things, whether it's coding, whether it's design,

450
00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:11.360
whether it's a bit of architecture work or whatever

451
00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:14.160
at the various stages, it's not always going to get it right.

452
00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:17.560
What, what, how do we manage

453
00:22:17.560 --> 00:22:21.320
to ensure that either the agent or the orchestrator

454
00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:26.160
is validating that what is being built, this is pretest.

455
00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:31.000
What is being built is being built to our guidance or our standards.

456
00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:34.280
Yeah. So twofold.

457
00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:37.440
One is we intentionally shift

458
00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:41.280
task ownership and task decomposition onto the human at the moment.

459
00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:42.440
So we're not saying

460
00:22:42.440 --> 00:22:46.480
give the Dark Factory your Q1 plans and it will go do all of that for you.

461
00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:49.880
We're saying give the Dark Factory will scope linear ticket like you would

462
00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:54.360
to a junior or mid-level engineer, and it will go implement that for you.

463
00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:57.600
And so there is an expectation that there has been a fair amount of design

464
00:22:57.600 --> 00:23:01.400
work, design thinking, potentially whiteboarding, potentially discussion

465
00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:05.080
beforehand, which you are then able to encode in the linear ticket,

466
00:23:05.080 --> 00:23:08.400
which then kind of guides the implementation after that.

467
00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:13.080
And so think of it as your job no longer is take ticket and go implement it.

468
00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:16.640
Your job is have the whiteboarding conversation with your colleagues.

469
00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:21.160
Capture that either as like a granola transcript or a screenshot or whatever,

470
00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:24.480
and then work with an agent to then translate that into a set of tickets

471
00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:26.720
that the factory can then go implement for you.

472
00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:30.640
So there's a little bit of that shift of prior work up front

473
00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:34.000
in terms of the actual does it follow what you do?

474
00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:38.720
There's one thing is implementation is a free or functionally free.

475
00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:39.960
You have to pay token costs.

476
00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:42.120
So kick off six, kick off seven.

477
00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:43.200
Like you've got a couple of ideas.

478
00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:46.640
Just go kick them off and don't send them an auto merge and go look at the code.

479
00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:50.160
Like I kind of almost view the fact that there is a potential

480
00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:53.760
like wide state space of potential solutions as either

481
00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:54.560
you don't care about it

482
00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:58.200
and so you should accept whatever satisfies it or be you're unsure.

